<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE mapper PUBLIC "-//mybatis.org//DTD Mapper 3.0//EN" "https://mybatis.org/dtd/mybatis-3-mapper.dtd">

<!--
  The charge engine. Two of these statements read the legacy tariff chain (CORP_CH_PC_DTL,
  COM_CH_PC_DTL and the COM_MT_CH_MTRX matrix behind both); the other three are ordinary
  statements against TRX_TASK_CHARGE, this service's own table (V12). This service creates
  none of the legacy tables and Flyway must never try.

  Legacy columns are NVARCHAR2, so every literal compared against one is written N'...' -
  a plain 'N' next to an NVARCHAR2 column raises ORA-12704 (character set mismatch). The
  TRX_TASK_CHARGE columns follow the same style, so the rule holds throughout this file.

  WHAT THE LIVE LEGACY DATA ACTUALLY LOOKS LIKE. The chain below was walked against the
  real schema on 2026-09-07, and the findings are worth writing down because the table
  names suggest the opposite of what is true.

  CORP_CH_PC_DTL IS THE AUTHORITATIVE TARIFF, NOT AN OVERRIDE. The name reads like an
  exception table for negotiated customers, and it is not: it holds 53,650 rows across 780
  companies in the SITLITE copy, and companies whose CORP.IS_SPC_CH is N'N' - the ones with
  no special charging at all - have rows there just the same. The mechanism is that the
  package is a TEMPLATE which the legacy procedure INJECT_CHARGE_COMPANY copies down into
  the per-company table when a company is set up. So the company row is the tariff that was
  actually applied, and the package is only what it was born from.

  THE FALLBACK IS NOT A CORNER CASE. 1,548 of the 2,303 active companies have no
  per-company row at all - the injection was never run for them - so a charge engine that
  reads only CORP_CH_PC_DTL finds nothing for two thirds of the estate. That is why
  findPackageTariffs exists and why the caller must actually use it.

  DUPLICATES ARE NORMAL AND THE SQL DOES NOT RESOLVE THEM. Neither table has a soft-delete
  column and neither has a unique key beyond ID, so both contain repeated (package or
  company, matrix detail, currency) keys: 92 in the package table, 282 in the company
  table. The typical pair is a 2015 row sitting beside a 2021 row with a different amount -
  the shape of an edit that was implemented as an insert. UPDATED_DT is therefore selected
  by both statements and the SERVICE picks the newest row per (charge type, currency); the
  SQL deliberately does not try to, because a FETCH FIRST or a MAX() here would bury the
  choice in a query plan where nobody would find it. Note plainly that this newest-wins
  rule is an INFERENCE awaiting confirmation from the legacy team: nothing in the database
  states which of a duplicate pair is live, and the alternative reading - that the legacy
  application picks by insertion order or by matrix detail - cannot be ruled out from the
  data alone.

  A TRANSFER CARRIES SEVERAL COMPONENTS, NEVER ONE. Every interbank service is billed as a
  generic transfer fee plus a rail-specific fee: LLG is Transfer Fee (COM_MT_CH_TYP.CD 014)
  plus LLG Fee (009), RTGS is 014 plus RTGS Fee (012), ONLINE is 014 plus Online Domestic
  Fee (020), and BI-Fast is 014 plus BI-FAST Domestic Fee (035). Both statements therefore
  return a LIST per service, and each element carries its own currency.

  CH_OC_CD PINS THE OCCASION. N'TRX' rows are per-transaction charges, which is all this
  engine is about; N'PRD' rows are periodical (monthly subscription-style) charges billed by
  a different process entirely, and letting one leak into a transaction's breakdown would
  charge a customer their monthly fee on every transfer. The guard is in the matrix join,
  alongside the matrix's own IS_DELETE / IS_INACTIVE flags and the charge type's.
-->
<mapper namespace="id.co.bni.direct.transaction.repository.mapper.ChargeMapper">

    <!--
      The matrix leg both tariff reads share: the matrix row pins the service and the
      occasion, the matrix DETAIL row names the charge type, and COM_MT_CH_TYP supplies the
      code and the display name. The two IS_ flags on each are the only "is this row still
      live" signal these tables have - the tariff tables themselves have none.
    -->
    <sql id="chargeMatrix">
        JOIN COM_MT_CH_MTRX m
          ON m.ID = o.CH_MTRX_ID
         AND m.SRVC_CD = #{srvcCd}
         AND m.CH_OC_CD = N'TRX'
         AND m.IS_DELETE = N'N'
         AND m.IS_INACTIVE = N'N'
        JOIN COM_MT_CH_MTRX_DTL md
          ON md.ID = o.CH_MTRX_DTL_ID
        JOIN COM_MT_CH_TYP t
          ON t.CD = md.CH_TYP_CD
         AND t.IS_DELETE = N'N'
         AND t.IS_INACTIVE = N'N'
    </sql>

    <!--
      The per-company tariff - the authoritative one. VAL_TYP rides along unconverted:
      N'F' rows carry a flat amount in VALUE, N'B' rows carry an id into a banded table and
      must be refused by the caller rather than parsed as a number (ChargeRows.TariffRow).
      UPDATED_DT is here for the duplicate resolution the service performs.
    -->
    <select id="findCompanyTariffs" resultType="id.co.bni.direct.transaction.entity.ChargeRows$TariffRow">
        SELECT t.CD         AS "chTypCd",
               t.NM         AS "chTypNm",
               o.CCY_CD     AS "ccyCd",
               o.VAL_TYP    AS "valTyp",
               o.VALUE      AS "value",
               o.UPDATED_DT AS "updatedDt"
        FROM CORP_CH_PC_DTL o
        <include refid="chargeMatrix"/>
        WHERE o.CORP_ID = #{companyId}
    </select>

    <!--
      The package template, used only as the fallback for the two thirds of companies with
      no row of their own. Entered from CORP because the package is not held on the tariff
      row: the company names a SERVICE package (CORP.SRVC_PC_CD), the service package names
      a CHARGE package (COM_SRVC_PC.CH_PC_CD), and the charge package is what COM_CH_PC_DTL
      hangs off. CORP.IS_DELETE is checked here because CORP has the soft-delete flag that
      the tariff tables lack.
    -->
    <select id="findPackageTariffs" resultType="id.co.bni.direct.transaction.entity.ChargeRows$TariffRow">
        SELECT t.CD         AS "chTypCd",
               t.NM         AS "chTypNm",
               o.CCY_CD     AS "ccyCd",
               o.VAL_TYP    AS "valTyp",
               o.VALUE      AS "value",
               o.UPDATED_DT AS "updatedDt"
        FROM CORP c
        JOIN COM_SRVC_PC sp
          ON sp.CD = c.SRVC_PC_CD
        JOIN COM_CH_PC_DTL o
          ON o.CH_PC_CD = sp.CH_PC_CD
        <include refid="chargeMatrix"/>
        WHERE c.ID = #{companyId}
          AND c.IS_DELETE = N'N'
    </select>

    <!-- One resolved component, frozen at submit. VERSION starts at 0 and CREATED_DT comes
         from SYSDATE so every environment stamps database time (the V1 convention). The
         re-quote columns are left NULL: they are release's business, never submit's. -->
    <insert id="insertCharge">
        INSERT INTO TRX_TASK_CHARGE (ID, TRX_TASK_ID, SEQ_NO, CH_TYP_CD, CH_TYP_NM,
                                     CCY_CD, AMT, IDR_AMT, FX_RATE, FX_RATE_TYPE,
                                     FX_RATE_SIDE, TARIFF_SOURCE, VERSION, CREATED_BY,
                                     CREATED_DT)
        VALUES (#{id}, #{trxTaskId}, #{seqNo}, #{chTypCd}, #{chTypNm},
                #{ccyCd}, #{amt}, #{idrAmt}, #{fxRate}, #{fxRateType},
                #{fxRateSide}, #{tariffSource}, 0, #{createdBy},
                SYSDATE)
    </insert>

    <!-- The breakdown behind TRX_TASK.FEE_AMT. Ordered by SEQ_NO so the components are
         presented in the same order on every screen that shows them. -->
    <select id="findCharges" resultType="id.co.bni.direct.transaction.entity.ChargeRows$ChargeRow">
        SELECT SEQ_NO           AS "seqNo",
               CH_TYP_CD        AS "chTypCd",
               CH_TYP_NM        AS "chTypNm",
               CCY_CD           AS "ccyCd",
               AMT              AS "amt",
               IDR_AMT          AS "idrAmt",
               FX_RATE          AS "fxRate",
               FX_RATE_TYPE     AS "fxRateType",
               FX_RATE_SIDE     AS "fxRateSide",
               TARIFF_SOURCE    AS "tariffSource",
               REQUOTED_IDR_AMT AS "requotedIdrAmt",
               REQUOTED_FX_RATE AS "requotedFxRate"
        FROM TRX_TASK_CHARGE
        WHERE TRX_TASK_ID = #{taskId}
        ORDER BY SEQ_NO
    </select>

    <!-- Release re-quoted this component. IDR_AMT, FX_RATE and AMT are NOT in the SET
         list, on purpose: what the approvers saw stays readable next to what was finally
         charged. REQUOTED_DT is SYSDATE for the same reason the other timestamps are. -->
    <update id="updateRequote">
        UPDATE TRX_TASK_CHARGE
        SET REQUOTED_IDR_AMT = #{requotedIdrAmt},
            REQUOTED_FX_RATE = #{requotedFxRate},
            REQUOTED_DT = SYSDATE,
            VERSION = VERSION + 1
        WHERE TRX_TASK_ID = #{taskId}
          AND SEQ_NO = #{seqNo}
    </update>
</mapper>
